725569d98ecd"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Wiener systems", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.783700003143545*^9, 
  3.783700007972226*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e2445571-a17b-459e-82bd-\
7bfd6a59febe"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A linear dynamic subsystem followed by a static nonlinearity (e.g., cuber, \
hard limiter, etc) is called a Wiener system after Norbert Wiener (see ",
 ButtonBox["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norbert_Wiener",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norbert_Wiener"], None},
  ButtonNote->"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norbert_Wiener"],
 "), the MIT mathematician who developed a number of system identification \
techniques. Wiener systems should not be confused with Wiener kernel \
nonlinear system representations. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.783700018028175*^9, 3.783700163503906*^9}, {
  3.7837126965203156`*^9, 3.783712709486042*^9}, {3.784201699383464*^9, 
  3.784201738599785*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c42933b6-3177-41cc-bd4a-\
f701c5170ead"],

