 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"b0fb3b8d-7c05-4d43-9bf4-45d31b984aae"],

Cell["\<\
Here we see a Wiener system consisting of a linear dynamic subsystem as \
represented by an impulse response function, h(\[Tau]), follow by a static \
nonlinear element, n(\[CenterDot]). The input to the Wiener system is denoted \
by, x(t), and the output from the Wiener system is denoted by, y(t). An \
internal signal u(t) is the output from the linear dynamic component, \
h(\[Tau]), and is also the input to the static nonlinear element, n(\
\[CenterDot]).
Sometimes Wiener systems are called LN systems because there is a linear \
dynamic system (L) followed by a static nonlinearity (N). 

An NL system consists of a nonlinear static element (N) followed by a linear \
dynamic element (L) and is called a Hammerstein system. Hammerstein system \
identification is the subject of another MICA Notebook.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7837001774699593`*^9, 3.783700645586067*^9}, {
   3.783712731510212*^9, 3.783712788064506*^9}, 3.783719425377431*^9, {
   3.784056238406757*^9, 3.784056268673308*^9}, {3.7840568203702817`*^9, 
