Cell["\<\
This discovery function returns suitable static models together with a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Score\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and a measure of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Complexity\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. A \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]good\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] model is one which has a \
high Score (a good fit) but a low level of complexity under the assumption \
that simplicity is important. Using these two criteria we choose the simple \
cuber model. A cuber is exactly the static nonlinearity used in the Wiener \
model we used. Furthermore  the single parameter of this model has a value \
near to 10 which is the same as that used when the Wiener system was \
specified previously. In other words we have identified the static \
nonlinearity.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7840551170974817`*^9, 3.784055361486759*^9}, {
  3.784055440114767*^9, 3.78405552007748*^9}, {3.784055571865853*^9, 
  3.784055627242165*^9}, {3.784055660406129*^9, 3.7840556812183466`*^9}, {
  3.784055752246482*^9, 3.784055810822974*^9}, {3.923395714754919*^9, 
  3.92339573520184*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fef842d2-88d8-41ac-ae7f-\
e89fc174d7f9"],

Cell["\<\
FindFormula does a quick fit and test of a wide range of functions. Lets redo \
the pure cuber fit:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.784413758414238*^9, 
  3.7844138317129536`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9600fdf8-02c4-4795-bb76-\
c9d1f4b7f95f"],

